    {
      "text": "%spark.ipyspark\n\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import IsolationForest\n\n# Assuming \u0027conditions\u0027 DataFrame is loaded\nconditions[\u0027start\u0027] \u003d pd.to_datetime(conditions[\u0027start\u0027])\n\n# Group by \u0027DESCRIPTION\u0027 and \u0027START\u0027 (day) to count occurrences\ncondition_counts \u003d conditions.groupby([\u0027description\u0027, pd.Grouper(key\u003d\u0027start\u0027, freq\u003d\u0027D\u0027)])[\u0027start\u0027].count().unstack()\n\n# Fill NaN with 0\ncondition_counts \u003d condition_counts.fillna(0)\n\n# Use IsolationForest for anomaly detection\nmodel \u003d IsolationForest(contamination\u003d0.01)  # Adjust contamination as needed\nmodel.fit(condition_counts)\nanomaly_scores \u003d model.decision_function(condition_counts)\n\n# Find conditions with significantly higher counts (anomalies)\ncondition_counts[\u0027Anomaly_Score\u0027] \u003d anomaly_scores\nanomalies \u003d condition_counts[condition_counts[\u0027Anomaly_Score\u0027] \u003c -0.01]  # Adjust threshold as needed\n\n# Print the descriptions of conditions with significantly rising counts\nprint(\"Conditions with Significantly Rising Counts (Anomalies):\")\nfor description in anomalies.index:\n  print(description)",
